  "slug": "fitness-dynamics-cb09",
  "summary": "New models add population structure, plasticity, spatial networks and function-valued traits to coexistence, virulence and fitness-landscape theory.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is theory that replaces fixed fitness values with structure-dependent evolutionary dynamics. A review frames evolution as fitness landscape navigation, relating epistasis, ruggedness and accessibility. Global epistasis models fitted to yeast data predict how fitness in non-home environments changes during adaptation. A multi-locus model shows that mutation rates evolve by indirect selection out of equilibrium and are selected against under homogenising selection. Two preprints extend modern coexistence theory, one by incorporating within-population structure (influenza substrains, perennial plants) and one by adding competition-induced plasticity, which alters niche and fitness differences. Host-parasite models find that degree heterogeneity in spatial networks drives host resistance and spatial variation in parasite infectivity, and that parasite-impaired host mobility favours reduced virulence through changes in relatedness and host density. Formal advances include adaptive dynamics for function-valued life-history traits on Hilbert spaces and continuous stationary distributions of sex allocation at selection-mutation balance.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
